# DELIVERABLES.md

Everything the club is graded on, taken from the project brief. A checklist, not background
reading.

---

## 1. Hard deadlines

| Item | Deadline |
| --- | --- |
| **Gantt chart** — all project stages with an expected completion date per stage | **5 days after the project is released** |
| **Final submission** — README, Trello board, video demonstration, code repository | **23:59, Wednesday 17 September 2026** |

The Gantt chart is due first and is the easiest to forget. The number of stages is the
team's choice.

---

## 2. Submission artifacts

- [ ] **Code repository** pushed to the club's official GitHub repo
- [ ] **README.md** (contents in §3)
- [ ] **Demo video** — uploading/recording a meeting, transcription in progress, and the
      final AI-generated summary output
- [ ] **Live deployed link** evaluators can actually use — note this means **both** the
      client (static host) **and** the server (long-running host) must be up
- [ ] **Trello board** reflecting real task tracking
- [ ] **Gantt chart**
- [ ] **OneDrive weekly reports**, linked from the repo

---

## 3. README.md — required sections

The brief lists these explicitly. A missing section loses marks for zero effort.
The README is for evaluators; `docs/` is for developers. Do not merge them.

1. **Purpose** — a short paragraph: what the tool does, why it was built, how it works at a
   high level.
2. **Setup instructions** — running it locally, start to finish, **for both `client/` and
   `server/`**, including the ffmpeg prerequisite. Condense `SETUP.md`.
3. **Library dependencies** — the notable ones and what each is for.
4. **Team contribution table** — must include **name, GitHub account, role**:

   | Name | GitHub | Role |
   | --- | --- | --- |
   | | | |

5. **`.env` format and what needs to be input** — both env tables from `SETUP.md` §3 with
   placeholder values, and a note that `server/.env` holds the secrets.
6. **Extra features description** — anything beyond the Core list.
7. Note that secrets live in `.env` and are never committed.

Put the live URL and the demo video link near the top.

---

## 4. Feature checklist — definition of done

### Core — all required
- [ ] Sign In / Sign Up
- [ ] Upload a meeting recording (audio **or video** file)
- [ ] Record directly in-browser
- [ ] View transcription progress
- [ ] Access the full generated transcript
- [ ] AI-generated summary in bullet-point form
- [ ] Automatic action item extraction — task, **owner**, **deadline**
- [ ] Speaker attribution — points labelled with who raised each one
- [ ] Topic tagging — bullets categorized (Decisions, Questions, Blockers, …)
- [ ] Export summaries as **Markdown**
- [ ] Export summaries as **PDF**
- [ ] Searchable history **by keyword** and **by date**

Three Core items are easy to under-deliver — check each explicitly:
- **Video file support** needs server-side ffmpeg audio extraction (`AI_INTEGRATION.md` §2)
  *and* ffmpeg present on the deploy host (`SETUP.md` §6).
- **Speaker attribution** — Whisper does not diarize (`AI_INTEGRATION.md` §4). Ship the
  inference plus the rename UI, and describe it accurately.
- **PDF export** must render Vietnamese diacritics (`ENDPOINTS.md` §6).

### Additional — stretch only
- [ ] Live summarization during an ongoing meeting
      (`LiveSession` + `/rt` namespace — `ARCHITECTURE.md` §3.3)

---

## 5. Suggested stages for the Gantt chart

Shift the dates to the real release date; the shape is what matters. Each stage ends with
something demonstrable, not "code written".

| Stage | Work | Exit criterion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Foundation | Repo layout, `client/` + `server/` scaffolds, module skeleton, shared middleware/envelope/event bus, Supabase project, migrations, auth + route guard, **both apps deployed** | A signed-in user sees an empty history page **on the live URLs**, with CORS and the socket handshake working |
| 2. Upload pipeline | Signed-URL upload, Dropzone, Recorder, `meeting` module CRUD, WebSocket progress plumbing | A file reaches Storage, a `meetings` row is `uploaded`, and the stepper updates over WebSocket |
| 3. Transcription | ffmpeg preprocessing, chunking, Whisper, `transcript` module, TranscriptView | A 30-minute **video** file produces a readable, correctly-timestamped transcript |
| 4. Summarization | Prompt, JSON parsing/normalization, `summary` + `action-item` modules, `overview` aggregator, detail page | Summary, tags, speakers, and action items all render from one request |
| 5. Features | Search + date filter, Markdown/PDF export, speaker rename, action item editing, three-state UI everywhere | Every Core checkbox ticked |
| 6. Polish & submit | Error/empty states, mobile, README, demo video, Trello, final deploy verification | Submission checklist complete |

Leave stage 6 at least a full week. Recording a good demo, writing the README, and fixing
whatever the first outside tester breaks always takes longer than planned.

Attempt the live-summarization stretch only if stage 5 finishes early.

---

## 6. Repository hygiene — explicitly graded

The brief asks for "clear and organized code structure", "proper file organization,
meaningful commit messages, and modular code". The architecture already delivers the
structure; keep the rest honest.

- **One repository**, `client/` and `server/` side by side. No nested `.git`.
- **Commit messages:** `type(scope): imperative summary` —
  `feat(transcript): chunk audio above the groq size limit`,
  `fix(summary): keep completed action items on resummarize`.
  Not `update`, not `fix bug`, not `asdf`.
- **Branches:** `main` always deployable; work on `feat/…` and `fix/…`, merge via PR.
- **No dead files.** No unused dependencies, no commented-out blocks, no scratch test
  scripts committed. The predecessor project shipped `fastapi`/`uvicorn` in
  `requirements.txt` while running Flask — reviewers notice.
- **No stale docs.** If a doc no longer matches the code, fix it or delete it. Documentation
  that contradicts the code was the clearest failure of the predecessor repo.
- **Module boundaries hold in review.** A PR that imports another module's service or
  repository directly gets sent back — that rule is the whole point of the architecture.

---

## 7. Demo video notes

Short, and show the real thing:

1. Sign in (10 s)
2. Upload a real recording — or record live, which is more convincing (30 s)
3. The progress stepper moving in real time (15 s)
4. The finished summary: bullets, category tags, speaker labels, action items (60 s)
5. Rename a speaker, tick an action item (15 s)
6. Search the history, export a PDF, open it (20 s)

Use a real meeting recording, not a 20-second test clip — a summary of a trivial input looks
trivial. Wake the backend first (`SETUP.md` §6). Have `MOCK_AI=true` output ready as a
fallback in case Groq rate-limits you mid-take.
</file>

<file path="docs/system.doc/AI_INTEGRATION.md">
# AI_INTEGRATION.md

The Groq layer: audio preprocessing, transcription, summarization, prompts, output parsing,
and the known gaps.

Placement follows the module boundaries. The Groq SDK client is a **config-layer** concern
(`server/src/config/groq.js`); the calls belong to the modules that own the resulting data:

| Concern | Lives in |
| --- | --- |
| Groq SDK client construction | `config/groq.js` |
| ffmpeg preprocessing + Whisper call | `modules/transcript/transcript.service.js` |
| Llama call, parsing, normalization | `modules/summary/summary.service.js` |
| Prompt text and `PROMPT_VERSION` | `modules/summary/summary.prompts.js` |
| Deciding *when* either runs | `modules/processing/` — via the event bus |

`GROQ_API_KEY` exists only in `server/`. If it ever appears in `client/`, that is a
security bug and a graded-project embarrassment.

---

## 1. Models

| Task | Model | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Speech → text | `whisper-large-v3` | Highest accuracy; handles Vietnamese. A `-turbo` variant is faster but less accurate — the brief names Large-v3, so use it. |
| Summarization | `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` | Large context window, supports JSON-mode output. |

Both via Groq's OpenAI-compatible API.

---

## 2. Audio preprocessing — `transcript` module

The backend is a long-running Node process, so **ffmpeg is installed and available**. All
media handling happens server-side; the client uploads the original file untouched.

Pipeline inside `transcript.service.js`:

1. **Download** the object from Supabase Storage using a signed URL.
2. **Extract audio** if the file is video (`.mp4`, `.webm`, `.mov`). This is what makes the
   brief's "upload a meeting recording (audio **or video** file)" requirement work — do not
   skip it and do not push it to the browser.
3. **Normalize** to 16 kHz mono FLAC. Whisper works at 16 kHz internally; anything more is
   wasted bytes on the wire.
   ```
   ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -vn -ac 1 -ar 16000 -c:a flac output.flac
   ```
4. **Chunk** only if the normalized file still exceeds Groq's per-request audio size limit
   (roughly 25 MB on the free tier; verify against current Groq docs and keep the number in
   one place as `GROQ_AUDIO_MAX_BYTES`). Split on ~10-minute boundaries, transcribe each
   piece, then concatenate — **applying a time offset to every segment's `start`/`end`**.
   Forgetting the offset silently corrupts every timestamp after the first chunk.
5. **Clean up** the temp files in a `finally` block. A long-running process leaks disk;
   a serverless one would not have.

> Chunking here exists only because of Groq's request size limit — not to dodge a platform
> timeout. That is the concrete benefit of `ARCHITECTURE.md` §0's deployment decision.

### Whisper call

```js
// verbose_json gives segment-level timestamps. We need them for jump-to-transcript
// and to ground speaker attribution in turn structure.
const res = await groq.audio.transcriptions.create({
  file: audioFile,
  model: 'whisper-large-v3',
  response_format: 'verbose_json',
  timestamp_granularities: ['segment'],
  language: undefined,   // auto-detect; meetings may be vi or en
  temperature: 0,
});
```

Persist `res.text` → `transcripts.full_text`, and the trimmed segments
(`{ id, start, end, text }` only) → `transcripts.segments`. Drop word-level data; it bloats
the row for no current feature.

On success, publish `transcript:created`. The `transcript` module must not know that
`summary` exists.

---

## 3. Summarization — `summary` module

### Input

Feed the model **numbered segments with timestamps**, not the flat blob:

```
[0 | 00:00:04] Okay so the deadline moved to the 17th.
[1 | 00:00:11] Right, then I'll redo the Gantt chart tonight.
```

It costs a few more tokens and buys the two things the brief requires: the model can cite
`startMs` per bullet, and it has the turn structure it needs to infer speakers.

If the transcript exceeds the context budget, map-reduce: summarize ~15-minute windows, then
run a merge pass. Keep the same output schema at both levels so one parser serves both.

### Call

```js
const completion = await groq.chat.completions.create({
  model: 'llama-3.3-70b-versatile',
  messages: [
    { role: 'system', content: SUMMARY_SYSTEM_PROMPT },
    { role: 'user',   content: buildTranscriptPrompt(segments) },
  ],
  response_format: { type: 'json_object' },
  temperature: 0.2,   // low: this is extraction, not creative writing
  max_tokens: 4096,
});
```

### Expected JSON

```json
{
  "overview": "2-3 sentence abstract of the meeting.",
  "speakers": [
    { "label": "Speaker 1", "evidence": "introduces themselves as the team lead" }
  ],
  "points": [
    { "content": "Deadline moved from Sept 10 to Sept 17.",
      "category": "decision", "speakerLabel": "Speaker 1", "startMs": 4000 }
  ],
  "actionItems": [
    { "content": "Redo the Gantt chart with the new deadline",
      "ownerLabel": "Speaker 2", "dueDate": "2026-09-12" }
  ]
}
```

`category` ∈ `decision | action | question | blocker | risk | discussion`.

### Parsing — defensive even in JSON mode

JSON mode reduces malformed output; it does not eliminate it, and it never guarantees *your*
schema. Layer the defences in `summary.service.js`:

```js
// 1. Strip markdown fences the model sometimes adds despite JSON mode.
// 2. Fall back to the outermost {...} span if there is stray prose around it.
// 3. Validate with Zod — well-formed JSON of the wrong shape is still a failure.
// 4. On Zod failure, retry ONCE with the validation errors appended to the prompt.
// 5. Still failing -> publish pipeline:failed. Never write partial rows.
```

Store the untouched string in `summaries.raw_response` **before** parsing. When a summary
looks wrong, that column is the only way to tell a bad prompt from a bad parser.

### Normalization rules

- Drop any action item with empty `content`.
- `dueDate`: accept only ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`. If the model returns "next Friday" or a past
  date, store `null` and keep the phrase inside `content`. **Never invent a date** — a wrong
  deadline in a meeting summary is worse than a missing one.
- A `speakerLabel` not present in the `speakers` array still creates a speaker row.
- Cap at 25 points and 20 action items; keep the first N and log the overflow.
- Action items are handed to the `action-item` module via the
  `summary:action-items-extracted` event, not by writing its table directly.

---

## 4. Speaker attribution — read before promising it

**Whisper does not do diarization.** It returns text with timestamps and no speaker IDs.
The brief requires speaker-labelled summary points, so the gap must be closed explicitly.

| Approach | How | Honest assessment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **A. LLM inference (chosen)** | Give Llama the timestamped segments; it infers distinct speakers from turn-taking, self-introductions, and addressing patterns. Emits `Speaker 1..N`, or a real name when someone is clearly named. | Free, no extra vendor, ships this week. Moderate accuracy, degrades with cross-talk. Adequate for a demo *if* users can correct it. |
| **B. User-corrected labels (chosen)** | A, plus `SpeakerLegend` + `PATCH /api/meetings/:id/speakers`. | Turns a shaky guess into a feature. **Build this regardless.** |
| **C. Real diarization** | pyannote / AssemblyAI / Deepgram produce speaker-labelled turns, then Llama summarizes them. | Genuinely accurate. Another vendor, another key, another failure mode, and it departs from the brief's "Groq API" line. Only if Core finishes early. |

**Decision: A + B.** In the README and demo video, call it *speaker attribution (inferred)*,
never "diarization". Overclaiming is easy to catch and costs more than it gains.

---

## 5. Prompts

All prompt text lives in `summary.prompts.js` as exported constants with a
`PROMPT_VERSION`. Never inline a prompt in a service method or a controller.

`SUMMARY_SYSTEM_PROMPT` must state, at minimum:

- Return a single JSON object matching the given schema. No prose, no markdown fences.
- Use only information present in the transcript. Omit what was not discussed.
- **Do not invent owners or deadlines.** Use `null` when unstated.
- Write the summary in the same language as the transcript.
- One factual sentence per bullet, no filler.
- Exactly one category per bullet.

Bump `PROMPT_VERSION` on any meaningful change so `summaries.prompt_version` records which
prompt produced which row.

---

## 6. Failure handling

Every failure publishes `pipeline:failed`; `processing` writes `status='failed'` plus a
readable `errorMessage` and emits `meeting:failed` over the socket.

| Failure | Handling |
| --- | --- |
| Groq `429` | Read `retry-after`, exponential backoff, max 3 attempts. Then `rate_limited` with `details.retryAfterMs`. |
| Timeout / `5xx` | 2 retries, then fail with "Transcription service unavailable, please retry". |
| Audio over the size limit | Chunk. Only fail if chunking is not implemented yet — and say so. |
| Empty transcript (silent recording) | Fail with "No speech detected in the recording". Do not run summarization on empty text. |
| Malformed JSON after one retry | Fail, keep `raw_response`, offer Retry in the UI. |
| ffmpeg non-zero exit | Fail with "Could not read the audio in this file". Log stderr server-side only. |

`errorMessage` must be readable by a non-technical evaluator. A raw stack trace in that
column is not acceptable.

---

## 7. Cost, rate limits, and demo insurance

Groq's free tier has per-minute and per-day request/token limits. Two practical consequences:

- **Never re-transcribe in a loop during development.** `POST /:id/process` returns `409`
  when a transcript already exists — that guard is a cost control, not just hygiene.
- **Keep fixtures.** Save one real transcript JSON and one summary JSON under
  `server/fixtures/`, and set `MOCK_AI=true` in `.env` to bypass Groq entirely. This lets
  the whole summarization and UI path be developed offline, and it is the fallback that
  saves the demo if the API rate-limits you mid-recording.

`MOCK_AI` is read in `config/groq.js` and swaps the client for a fixture-backed stub. No
module method changes — that is the point of putting the SDK in the config layer.
</file>

<file path="docs/system.doc/ARCHITECTURE.md">
# ARCHITECTURE.md

**NCT Meeting Summarizer** — architecture of record.

This document merges the team's standing architectural style (client–server split, backend
Modular Monolith, N-Tier layering on both sides, REST + WebSocket dual protocol,
package-based frontend components) with the concrete requirements of the NCT Meeting
Summarizer brief (Supabase Postgres/Auth/Storage, Groq Whisper + Llama, live deployed link).

Where the two disagreed, §0 records the decision and the reason.

---

## 0. Reconciled decisions

| Point of conflict | Decision | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Team style says "managed document database"; the brief mandates Supabase Postgres | **Supabase Postgres.** | The brief requires Supabase for DB, Auth, and Storage — it is a graded requirement. The repository layer exists precisely so this swap costs nothing above it: services and controllers are unchanged. |
| The "persistence transform" convention (rename `_id`, drop `__v`) is document-DB specific | **Kept, repurposed.** Postgres rows already expose a clean `id`. The convention now means: one shared DTO helper strips internal fields (`user_id`, `storage_path`, `raw_response`, error internals) uniformly before anything leaves the service layer. | Same principle preserved — one project-wide serialization rule, not a per-module decision. |
| Serverless functions vs a long-running Node process | **Long-running Node process.** | Removes the two hardest problems in this project: wall-clock timeouts on long transcriptions, and no ffmpeg in a serverless runtime. Media processing needs a real process. |
| Status polling vs WebSocket | **WebSocket primary, `GET /status` retained as fallback.** | Matches the team's protocol split, gives a live progress UI for free, and is the foundation the live-meeting stretch feature needs. Polling stays for reconnect/refresh, where a fresh socket has no history. |
| RLS as the authorization boundary | **Authorization enforced in the service/repository layer; RLS kept as defence in depth.** | The backend holds the service-role key, which bypasses RLS. Every repository method therefore takes `userId` and scopes the query on it. RLS still protects against a leaked anon key. |
| Modules should own their models | **Kept, with three model-less modules** (`processing`, `overview`, `export`). | The team style already permits orchestration/aggregation modules with no persistent model. This project needs exactly that shape for its pipeline and its composed read endpoints. |

---

## 1. Overall style

- **Client–Server.** `client/` and `server/` are separate codebases with separate
  dependency trees and build pipelines, deployed and versioned independently, coupled only
  through the REST + WebSocket contract.
- **Modular Monolith** on the backend: one deployable Node process, internally partitioned
  into bounded-context modules that own their own data and business rules.
- **N-Tier (Layered)** on both sides, at different granularity — backend layered *within
  each module*, frontend layered *within each component package*.
- **Dual protocol.** REST for request/response and CRUD; WebSocket for continuous,
  stateful, bidirectional interaction.

```
┌──────────────────────────────┐       REST /api/*        ┌───────────────────────────────┐
│  client/  (React SPA)        │ ◄──────────────────────► │  server/ (Node, long-running) │
│  static build on CDN host    │                          │  HTTP + WebSocket, one process│
│                              │ ◄──── WebSocket /rt ────► │                               │
└──────────────────────────────┘                          └───────────┬───────────────────┘
             │                                                        │
             │  direct upload via signed URL                          │
             ▼                                                        ▼
      ┌──────────────────┐                             ┌────────────────────┬────────────┐
      │ Supabase Storage │ ◄── download for processing │ Supabase Postgres  │  Groq API  │
      │ (private bucket) │                             │ (managed, external)│ Whisper +  │
      └──────────────────┘                             │                    │ Llama 3.3  │
                                                       └────────────────────┴────────────┘
```

Media bytes never traverse the API. The browser uploads straight to Supabase Storage using
a short-lived signed URL issued by the server; the server downloads the object when it is
ready to process it.

---

## 2. Backend — Modular Monolith

### 2.1 Module boundary rules

- A module may **not** import another module's internal service, repository, or model.
- Cross-module communication happens only through:
  1. the module's public **interface** (`*.interface.js` — an explicit exported contract), or
  2. the decoupled **event bus** (publish/subscribe), for fire-and-forget side effects.
- A module may own no persistent model at all, existing purely as an
  **orchestration/aggregation layer** composing other modules' interfaces.

### 2.2 Modules

| Module | Owns | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `auth` | — (delegates to Supabase Auth) | Verifies Supabase JWTs, resolves the request user, exposes `requireAuth`. |
| `user` | `Profile` | Display name, avatar, account settings. |
| `meeting` | `Meeting` | The durable meeting record. Owns creation as an empty `draft` card and the `draft → uploaded` transition when a file is attached later. Everything from `uploaded` onward belongs to `processing`. |
| `transcript` | `Transcript` | Audio preprocessing (ffmpeg) + Groq Whisper + segment storage. |
| `summary` | `Summary`, `SummaryPoint`, `Speaker` | Groq Llama call, JSON parsing/normalization, bullets, topic tags, speaker labels. |
| `action-item` | `ActionItem` | CRUD + completion state. Seeded from summary output, then user-owned. |
| `live-session` | `LiveSession` (transient, TTL) | Real-time recording session; materializes into durable records on end. |
| `processing` | — | **Orchestrator.** Drives the AI pipeline (`uploaded` onward), publishes progress events. |
| `overview` | — | **Aggregator.** Builds composed meeting-detail and history-list responses from the interfaces above. |
| `export` | — | Renders Markdown and PDF from `overview`'s composed payload. |

`processing`, `overview`, and `export` are the model-less modules. They are where the
project's cross-cutting write and read flows live, so no module ever reaches into another's
internals.

### 2.3 Directory layout

```
server/
├── src/
│   ├── index.js                     # single entry: bootstraps HTTP + WebSocket together
│   ├── app.js                       # express app assembly, middleware chain, route mounting
│   │
│   ├── config/                      # global config layer — outside the module/tier structure
│   │   ├── database.js              # Postgres connection
│   │   ├── supabase.js              # service-role client (DB + Storage)
│   │   ├── groq.js                  # Groq SDK client
│   │   ├── upload.js                # signed-URL issuance, MIME/size allowlists
│   │   ├── socket.js                # WebSocket server + namespace registration
│   │   └── swagger.js               # OpenAPI generation
│   │
│   ├── middleware/                  # shared middleware layer
│   │   ├── authenticate.js          # verifies Supabase JWT → req.user
│   │   ├── authorize.js             # role checks
│   │   ├── validate.js              # runs a module's validator schema
│   │   ├── rateLimit.js
│   │   └── errorHandler.js          # centralized; the ONLY place that formats a failure envelope
│   │
│   ├── shared/                      # shared utilities layer
│   │   ├── eventBus.js
│   │   ├── response.js              # success()/failure() envelope + paginate()
│   │   ├── errors.js                # AppError hierarchy with machine codes
│   │   ├── dto.js                   # the project-wide serialization transform
│   │   ├── constants.js             # statuses, categories, limits
│   │   └── logger.js
│   │
│   └── modules/
│       ├── auth/  user/  meeting/  transcript/  summary/
│       ├── action-item/  live-session/
│       └── processing/  overview/  export/
└── package.json
```

### 2.4 Internal N-Tier flow

Within each module, dependencies flow strictly in one direction:

```
Route / Socket Handler → Controller → Service → Repository / Interface → Model
```

A full module package:

```
meeting/
├── meeting.model.js         # schema definition / table mapping
├── meeting.repository.js    # ALL data access. Every method takes userId and scopes on it.
├── meeting.service.js       # business rules, status transitions, event publishing
├── meeting.controller.js    # HTTP-shaped: reads req, calls service, returns envelope
├── meeting.routes.js        # path → middleware chain → controller
├── meeting.validator.js     # inbound boundary: request schema validation
├── meeting.dto.js           # outbound boundary: response shaping, strips internal fields
├── meeting.interface.js     # public contract other modules may call
└── meeting.socket.js        # optional: this module's real-time handlers
```

Non-negotiable:
- Routes and socket handlers never touch models.
- Services never bypass the repository.
- Validators run **before** controller logic. Controllers assume valid input.
- DTOs run **before** data leaves the service layer. Nothing raw from a repository reaches
  a controller-consumable surface.
- `meeting.interface.js` exposes only what other modules genuinely need
  (`getMeetingForOwner`, `assertOwnership`, `markFailed`, …) — never the whole service.

### 2.5 Cross-cutting conventions

**Response envelope.** Exactly two shapes, project-wide:

```jsonc
// success
{ "success": true, "data": { }, "meta": { } }

// failure — produced only by errorHandler.js
{ "success": false, "error": { "code": "validation_failed", "message": "…", "details": { } } }
```

**Pagination.** Every list endpoint, identically:

```jsonc
{ "success": true,
  "data": [ ],
  "meta": { "page": 1, "limit": 20, "total": 37, "totalPages": 2 } }
```

**Serialization transform.** `shared/dto.js` provides the one rule every module's DTO
composes with: drop `user_id`, `storage_path`, `raw_response`, and any error internals;
serialize timestamps as ISO strings. A Storage path or a raw provider response must never
reach a client.

**Errors.** Modules throw typed `AppError`s carrying a machine code. Only `errorHandler.js`
converts them into a response. No `res.status(400).json(...)` inside a controller.

---

## 3. Protocol split

### 3.1 REST — request/response

Auth callbacks, profile management, meeting CRUD, paginated history, keyword/date search,
transcript and summary retrieval, action-item CRUD, speaker renaming, exports, and one-off
state snapshots (including `GET /api/meetings/:id/status`).

Full contract: `ENDPOINTS.md`.

### 3.2 WebSocket — continuous/stateful

A single namespace, `/rt`, hosts all real-time events. Convention: `namespace:action`, with
structured object payloads in both directions.

**Connection.** The client connects with its Supabase access token in the handshake auth
payload. The server verifies it through the `auth` module and joins the socket to a private
room `user:{userId}`. An unverified socket is disconnected immediately — there is no
anonymous real-time surface.

**Server → client**

| Event | Payload | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `meeting:progress` | `{ meetingId, status, step, progress }` | Every pipeline state transition |
| `meeting:completed` | `{ meetingId }` | Pipeline reaches `completed` |
| `meeting:failed` | `{ meetingId, code, message }` | Pipeline reaches `failed` |
| `live:transcript-chunk` | `{ sessionId, seq, text, startMs, endMs }` | A live chunk finished transcribing |
| `live:summary-updated` | `{ sessionId, overview, points }` | Rolling summary refreshed |
| `live:ended` | `{ sessionId, meetingId }` | Live session materialized into a durable meeting |

**Client → server**

| Event | Payload | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `meeting:subscribe` | `{ meetingId }` | Join the room for one meeting's progress |
| `meeting:unsubscribe` | `{ meetingId }` | Leave it |
| `live:start` | `{ title, meetingDate }` | Open a `LiveSession` |
| `live:chunk` | `{ sessionId, seq, blob }` | Push an audio chunk |
| `live:end` | `{ sessionId }` | Close and materialize |

**Presence.** Connect/disconnect are tracked per user so a live session survives a reconnect
instead of being lost.

### 3.3 Transient vs durable state

This is the model that makes the brief's "Additional" feature tractable.

| | Transient | Durable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Entity | `LiveSession` | `Meeting` + `Transcript` + `Summary` |
| Mutability | Mutated many times per minute (chunks appended, rolling summary replaced) | Immutable once written, except explicit user edits |
| Lifetime | Time-boxed: `expiresAt`, swept by a periodic job | Durable |
| Driven by | WebSocket events | REST |

On `live:end`, `live-session` calls the `meeting`, `transcript`, and `summary` interfaces to
materialize one durable record set, emits `live:ended`, and marks the session expired. If a
session expires without a clean end, the sweeper materializes whatever exists and marks the
meeting `completed`, or `failed` if there is no usable transcript. **A dropped connection
must never silently lose a recording.**

---

## 4. The processing pipeline

A meeting moves through two owners. `meeting` itself creates the card and owns the single
`draft → uploaded` transition once a file is attached, from the meeting's own page — a
meeting can sit in `draft` indefinitely with no file at all. From `uploaded` onward,
`processing` is the only module that writes `meeting.status`, doing so through the
`meeting` interface.

```
 client                          meeting
   │  POST /api/meetings              │
   ├─────────────────────────────────►│ create() → status=draft
   │  ◄──────────── 201, no file ─────┤
   │                                  │
   │   (user opens the meeting page, picks or records a file — could be any time later)
   │                                  │
   │  POST /:id/upload                │
   ├─────────────────────────────────►│ status=uploaded
   │  ◄──────── signed upload URL ────┤
   │                                  │
   ├── PUT file → Supabase Storage (direct) ────────────►
```

From here, `processing` takes over:

```
 client                    processing            transcript             summary
   │  POST /:id/process         │                     │                     │
   ├───────────────────────────►│ status=transcribing │                     │
   │  ◄──── 202 Accepted ───────┤ publish             │                     │
   │                            │  meeting:uploaded ─►│ download + ffmpeg   │
   │  ◄═ ws meeting:progress ═══╡                     │ Groq Whisper        │
   │                            │◄─ transcript:created┤ status=transcribed  │
   │  ◄═ ws meeting:progress ═══╡ status=summarizing ──────────────────────►│ Groq Llama
   │                            │                     │                     │ parse+normalize
   │                            │◄──────────────── summary:created ─────────┤
   │                            │ publish summary:action-items-extracted ──► action-item
   │  ◄═ ws meeting:completed ══╡ status=completed    │                     │
```

Event bus contracts:

| Event | Publisher | Subscribers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `meeting:uploaded` | `processing` | `transcript` |
| `transcript:created` | `transcript` | `processing` |
| `summary:created` | `summary` | `processing` |
| `summary:action-items-extracted` | `summary` | `action-item` |
| `pipeline:failed` | any stage | `processing` (status write + socket emit) |
| `meeting:deleted` | `meeting` | storage cleanup, `transcript`, `summary`, `action-item` |

Why the event bus rather than direct calls: `transcript` must not know that `summary`
exists, and neither should know anything about WebSocket emission. `processing` subscribes
to everything and is the single place translating domain events into status transitions and
socket messages.

### 4.1 State machine

`meeting.status` is the single source of truth. Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`.

```
draft ──► uploaded ──► transcribing ──► transcribed ──► summarizing ──► completed
              │              │                │               │
              └──────────────┴────────────────┴───────────────┴────► failed  (+ errorMessage)
```

- `draft → uploaded` is written by `meeting` itself when a file is attached
  (`ENDPOINTS.md` §2). Every transition from `uploaded` onward is written by `processing`.
- Forward-only, or to `failed`. Never backwards. `draft` never fails — there's nothing to
  fail yet; a meeting stuck in `draft` is just waiting for a file, not broken.
- Every transition to `failed` writes a message a non-technical evaluator can read —
  never a raw stack trace.
- Retry re-enters at the earliest incomplete stage: if a transcript already exists, retry
  only re-runs summarization.
- `progress` in the socket payload is a fixed lookup per status (`draft` 0, `uploaded` 0.1
  → `completed` 1.0). It exists so the bar moves; it is not a measurement.

### 4.2 What the long-running process buys us

Because the backend is a persistent Node process rather than a serverless function:

- **ffmpeg is available.** Video → audio extraction and 16 kHz mono downsampling happen
  server-side, in `transcript`. The client uploads the original file untouched.
- **No wall-clock ceiling.** A 60-minute recording can transcribe in one pass. Chunking is
  still used when a file exceeds Groq's per-request audio size limit — for that reason
  only, not to dodge a platform timeout.
- **In-process job state.** The pipeline runs in the same process that holds the WebSocket
  connections, so progress emission needs no external queue or pub/sub broker.

Trade-off accepted: a single process is a single point of failure and will not scale
horizontally without an external queue. At this project's scale that is the right trade,
and the seam is already in place — replace the in-memory event bus with a durable queue and
no module code changes.

---

## 5. Frontend

### 5.1 Layers

```
Pages  →  Components  →  Hooks / Services  →  Global Stores / Utils
```

- **Pages** — composition root, grouped by access tier.
- **Components** — presentation + local interaction logic.
- **Hooks** — stateful logic and event handlers, decoupled from JSX.
- **Services** (component-scoped) — outbound API calls for that specific component.
- **Global stores** — session/auth state, socket connection state, UI/theme state.
- **Global utils/config** — helpers and the single source of truth for backend endpoint
  definitions, usable by any layer without violating dependency direction.

### 5.2 Layout

```
client/
├── src/
│   ├── main.jsx
│   ├── App.jsx
│   ├── routes/
│   │   ├── routeTable.js            # centralized URL → page map
│   │   └── RouteGuard.jsx           # role-based gate, mirrors backend authorize.js
│   ├── pages/
│   │   ├── public/                  # Landing, Login, Signup, AuthCallback
│   │   └── authenticated/           # MeetingHistory, NewMeeting, MeetingDetail, Settings
│   ├── components/
│   │   ├── Dropzone/  Recorder/  ProcessingStatus/
│   │   ├── SummaryView/  TranscriptView/  ActionItemList/
│   │   ├── SpeakerLegend/  ExportMenu/  LiveSessionPanel/
│   ├── stores/
│   │   ├── auth.store.js
│   │   ├── socket.store.js
│   │   └── ui.store.js
│   ├── hooks/                       # global hooks only (useSocketEvent, useAuth)
│   ├── config/
│   │   ├── endpoints.js             # SINGLE source of truth for backend paths
│   │   └── env.js                   # validated import.meta.env access
│   └── utils/
└── vite.config.js
```

### 5.3 Package-based componentization

Each component is a self-contained package, not a file:

```
ProcessingStatus/
├── index.jsx                        # presentation (JSX) only
├── sub-components/                  # children private to this package
│   ├── StatusStepper.jsx
│   └── FailureNotice.jsx
├── useProcessingStatus.hook.js      # logic, socket subscription, fallback polling
├── processingStatus.service.js      # API calls scoped to this component
└── ProcessingStatus.css
```

A component is exposed to the rest of the app **only** through its `index.jsx`. Nothing
outside the package imports its hook, service, or sub-components.

An `index.jsx` containing an API call, or a hook file containing JSX, is a layering
violation and should be caught in review.

### 5.4 Routing and access control

- `routes/routeTable.js` maps every path to a page and an access tier.
- `RouteGuard.jsx` enforces the tier before a page renders: `public` (redirects signed-in
  users away from Login/Signup) and `authenticated` (redirects to Login otherwise). A third
  `elevated` tier is scaffolded but unused — the brief defines no admin role.
- The guard is a UX affordance, not security. **Every rule it enforces is enforced again in
  `middleware/authorize.js`.** A client-side guard alone protects nothing.

### 5.5 Real-time on the client

`socket.store.js` owns exactly one socket connection for the whole app, created after
authentication and torn down on sign-out. Components never construct their own socket.

`useSocketEvent(event, handler)` subscribes and unsubscribes with the component lifecycle.

`ProcessingStatus` subscribes to `meeting:progress` / `meeting:completed` / `meeting:failed`.
If the socket is disconnected, its hook **falls back to polling `GET /api/meetings/:id/status`
every 3 s** and stops as soon as the socket returns. The UI renders from the state machine,
so it behaves identically under either transport.

---

## 6. Deployment

| Piece | Target | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `client/` | Static SPA build on a CDN/static host (Vercel/Netlify) | All routes rewritten to `index.html` for client-side routing. This is the "live deployed link" the brief requires. |
| `server/` | One long-running Node process (Render / Railway / Fly.io) | HTTP + WebSocket bootstrapped from the same entry point. Requires a host that supports persistent connections — a serverless host breaks both WebSocket and the pipeline. |
| Database | Supabase Postgres — managed, external | Not colocated with either app. |
| Object storage | Supabase Storage, private bucket | Not colocated with either app. |

Independently deployed and versioned; coupled only through the REST/WebSocket contract.

Consequences to handle explicitly:
- **CORS.** The server allowlists the client origin from config — the dev origin locally,
  the deployed origin in production. Never `*`.
- **Socket origin.** The WebSocket server needs the same allowlist.
- **Cold start.** Free tiers on Render/Railway sleep idle instances; the first request after
  sleep can take 30–60 s. Wake the backend before recording the demo video and before the
  evaluators open the live link.

---

## 7. Principles summary

| Principle | Applied as |
| --- | --- |
| Separation of concerns | N-Tier layering — backend per-module, frontend per-component package |
| Bounded contexts | Modular Monolith; one owning module per model; model-less orchestration modules for cross-cutting flows |
| Encapsulation across boundaries | Modules reachable only via `*.interface.js` or the event bus; components only via `index.jsx` |
| Protocol-appropriate communication | REST for CRUD/snapshots, WebSocket for pipeline progress and live sessions |
| Data shape consistency | One response envelope, one pagination shape, one serialization transform, project-wide |
| Transient vs durable state | `LiveSession` (TTL, mutable) materializes into `Meeting`/`Transcript`/`Summary` (durable, immutable) |
| Defence in depth | Authorization in service + repository; RLS retained; route guard mirrored by backend middleware |
| Failure is a first-class state | `failed` + human-readable `errorMessage` at every pipeline stage, surfaced over both protocols |
</file>

<file path="docs/system.doc/LESSONS_FROM_TUTORBOT.md">
# LESSONS_FROM_TUTORBOT.md

The predecessor project ("TutorBot" / EduAssist) solved a structurally similar problem:
upload media → stage it in object storage → async transcription → LLM analysis returning a
JSON summary + action items → typed frontend client.

That pipeline shape is proven and is carried over. Most implementation details around it are
not. This file records both, so the same bugs are not paid for twice.

---

## 1. Patterns worth reusing

| Pattern from TutorBot | How it maps here |
| --- | --- |
| **Stage the file in object storage first, then transcribe** (NCP Object Storage → Clova Speech) | Supabase Storage → Groq Whisper. Media never passes through the API. |
| **Async processing with a client-visible status** (`/api/lectures/status`) | The `meeting.status` state machine, pushed over WebSocket with `GET /:id/status` as fallback. Same idea, better transport. |
| **LLM returns JSON with `summary` + `action_items`, defensively extracted from markdown fences** | Exactly the contract in `AI_INTEGRATION.md` §3. Keep the defensive extraction — Groq JSON mode helps but does not remove the need. |
| **A single typed client module wrapping backend calls** (`lib/api.ts`, `API_ENDPOINTS`) | `client/src/config/endpoints.js` + `utils/http.js`, consumed by component-scoped services. Kept and made mandatory. |
| **Action items as first-class rows with their own CRUD** | Kept. Users tick them off; they cannot live inside a JSON blob. |
| **A search endpoint over the content records** | `GET /api/meetings?q=&from=&to=`, upgraded to real Postgres full-text search. |

---

## 2. Concrete mistakes not to repeat

Each is a real, named problem from the predecessor repo, paired with the rule that prevents
it here.

### Hardcoded hosts in source
`config.ts`'s `apiUrl` was hardcoded to a remote nip.io host instead of reading
`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` — which existed in `.env.local` but was never actually read. The
Google OAuth redirect URI was likewise hardcoded in `auth.py`, contradicting its own setup doc.

**Rule:** every URL comes from `config/env.js` on the relevant side; every backend path comes
from `client/src/config/endpoints.js`. `grep -rn "https://" client/src server/src` should
surface no application host.

### Stale documentation shipped alongside the code
`AUTH_README.md` and `API_INTEGRATION_STATUS.md` described endpoints (`/api/register`,
`/api/login`, `/api/start_session`) that no longer existed. New contributors had to be told
"do not trust the docs."

**Rule:** an endpoint or socket-event change and its `ENDPOINTS.md` edit are one commit. If a
doc becomes untrue and you cannot fix it now, delete it.

### No migrations
Tables were auto-created by `Base.metadata.create_all` at startup. Schema changes were
invisible in git and unreproducible.

**Rule:** every schema change is a file in `server/supabase/migrations/`.

### Two nested git repositories
`tutor-bot/.git` inside the outer `TutorBot` repo, so contributors had to check which repo
they were committing to.

**Rule:** one repo, `client/` and `server/` side by side.

### A dependency file that lied
`requirements.txt` listed `fastapi` and `uvicorn` for an app that ran Flask.

**Rule:** each `package.json` contains only what is imported. Run a depcheck before submission.

### Ad-hoc fetch calls bypassing the API client
`lib/api.ts` existed, but most pages hand-rolled `fetch` with `getAuthHeaders()` /
`handleAuthError()`, so 401 handling was duplicated and inconsistent.

**Rule:** no `fetch` outside `utils/http.js`. Network calls live in a component package's
`*.service.js`. One centralized 401 handler.

### Dead code presented as live
An elaborate `components/socratic/*` UI kit sat unused while the app rendered the `simple/`
variants — nobody could tell what was real without reading imports.

**Rule:** delete unused components. Git remembers them. Package-based componentization makes
a dead component a dead *folder*, which is easy to spot and easy to remove.

### God components
`src/app/page.tsx` was one client component holding the sidebar, session list, drag-and-drop
upload, and the chat pane.

**Rule:** the component package structure enforces the split — JSX in `index.jsx`, logic in
the hook, network in the service. If a hook passes ~150 lines, the component is doing too
much. `NewMeeting` is the page most at risk of becoming this.

### Two parallel systems that shared nothing
`GeneralChatSession` and `SocraticChatSession` were separate table sets disambiguated by a
`?mode=` query param — double the schema, double the routes, no shared behaviour.

**Rule:** if two features need different tables *and* different endpoints, they are different
features. Ship one properly. Note the contrast with `LiveSession` here: it is a *transient
projection* of the same durable records, materialized into them, not a parallel universe.

### Hand-mirrored schemas
Pydantic schemas manually mirrored the SQLAlchemy models with no shared codegen; they drifted.

**Rule:** one Zod schema per boundary, in the module's `*.validator.js`. One serialization
transform in `shared/dto.js`. Never hand-map a field that a shared transform already handles.

### Layers that leaked
Routes reached models, `get_db()` existed but was not wired into the request lifecycle so
every route opened and closed its own session by hand.

**Rule:** `Route/Socket → Controller → Service → Repository/Interface → Model`, one
direction, no exceptions. Connection handling lives in `config/database.js`, not in route code.

### No tests at all
`test_storage.py` / `test_upload.txt` were ad-hoc scripts, not a suite.

**Rule:** full coverage is not expected on a club project, but write unit tests for the two
things that will break silently — the **LLM JSON parser/normalizer** and the **Markdown
export builder**. Both are pure functions and both are load-bearing.

---

## 3. Explicitly dropped from TutorBot

Not in the NCT brief. Do not port these, and do not let them creep back in:

- Socratic tutoring / quiz flow, and the general chat surface
- Chat sessions and message history of any kind
- Classes → Lectures hierarchy (a meeting here is a flat, standalone record)
- The calendar/scheduling feature with its own local data model and ICS export
- Per-action-item notebook pages
- Custom JWT auth, bcrypt, and hand-wired Google OAuth — Supabase Auth replaces all of it
- Flask, SQLAlchemy, MySQL, `docker-compose.yml`, nginx reverse-proxy config

Dropping the entire auth layer and swapping the backend framework is not a loss. It removes
roughly half the surface area of the old project along with every bug that lived there.

---

## 4. One line

**Keep the pipeline. Rebuild the plumbing, and keep the layers honest.**
</file>

